#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158290} :activity/id #uuid "55c2127a-d43f-53c5-907e-c8d12316517c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048747 :topic/id #uuid "bac6604a-5262-5144-8ceb-19f17d508317" :topic/math-academy-id 2987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158562 :tutorial/content "Suppose $X$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$.\n\nThe values $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment** of $X$, respectively.\n\nRecall that the first moment $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is also called the *expected value* of $X$ and is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo find second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$, the expected value of the the random variable $X^2$, we simply substitute $x^2$ for $x$ in the formula above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's compute the second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ of the continuous random variable $X$ with the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{S}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{5/2}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}x^{7/2})\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2847fd82-e842-5ae8-889a-66f29c0442ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6591 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3dc1ae12-0989-5596-8114-7d74427919d2" :step/math-academy-id 39753 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157393 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156994 :question/id #uuid "65957407-b302-51c5-93d9-0f71f3517362" :question/math-academy-id "e-10527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c799d6-e3fe-5b78-986d-f2068b2db058" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157991}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "897313ee-485e-51e5-8fec-b665d482b874" :step/math-academy-id 39754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157394 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156995 :question/id #uuid "292610ec-9c27-590d-b207-c76adc959d55" :question/math-academy-id "e-10528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bada6a7f-c02e-51cd-8ea2-0594a94648a1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049801}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157603}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "08325c1e-fe0c-5a7b-b6e5-8f27c2951461" :step/math-academy-id 39755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157395 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156996 :question/id #uuid "4c5666a4-f477-5b1a-8d17-1e2aa594863f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10529"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88bd32d5-4a84-58b3-a3e9-73bf32cb5e4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157605}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Higher Moments of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "bce87273-1a01-58a6-825f-736ef62ef788" :step/math-academy-id 39756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158563 :tutorial/content "It's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm{E}[X^3]$ is the **third moment** of the random variable $X$.\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\displaystyle \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *first* moment\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\displaystyle \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *second* moment\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^3] = \\displaystyle \\int_S x^3 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *third* moment\n\nand so on. In general, the **$n$th moment** is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFinally, note that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^2], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^3], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moments.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." :tutorial/id #uuid "e3527d5e-b0d3-5d76-aa5f-5bc7ffcfd66b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6592 :tutorial/title "Higher Moments"} :step/id #uuid "9fb3611b-a9ba-58f4-9159-00a7c9b29393" :step/math-academy-id 39757 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158293}}] :activity/title "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157533}]}
